[
  {
    "prompt": "Shot with a Canon EOS R5, 85mm f/1.8 lens. The composition features two glass mason jars, one sharply in focus and the other slightly blurred behind it, displayed on a rustic, aged oak wood table. The camera angle is a 3/4 overhead view, capturing the distinct, vibrant layers of a Southwestern salad. The bottom of the jars shows the translucent, golden emulsion of lime juice, olive oil, cumin, and flecks of green cilantro. Above the dressing, layers are clearly defined: halved bright red cherry tomatoes, dark black beans, sweet yellow corn kernels, and fluffy, ivory-colored cooked quinoa. Resting on the grains, the chopped, tender, poached chicken breast pieces are visible, and the top is packed with crisp, vibrant green chopped romaine lettuce. Shallow depth of field creates a natural bokeh. Soft natural side lighting from a window on the left illuminates the textures, creating a subtle sheen on the oil droplets and fresh produce. A small, used wooden spoon sits casually on the table next to a scattered half-lime and a few loose cilantro leaves. The scene appears lived-in, featuring a slight condensation on the glass and a small, authentic spill of a few grains of quinoa on the wood surface. The color palette is warm and inviting, defined by the contrast of fresh greens, reds, and earthy tones. Photorealistic, not AI-generated looking. The scene should look like someone just finished preparing a home-cooked lunch—warm, authentic, and not a sterile studio setup. Slight natural imperfections, such as an asymmetric layering, are present. No text, no watermark, no logo, no hands, no faces. The image is indistinguishable from a photograph taken by a professional food photographer for a printed cookbook."
  }
]
